  "We search our server logs with Python regexes; {WS}/server.log shows what a log looks like. We need one regex that gives, for every log line containing an IPv4 address, the last date on that line (YYYY-MM-DD); lines without an address give nothing. Only real calendar dates count, with February allowed up to the 29th in every year. Addresses are dotted decimal, each part 0 to 255 with no leading zeros. A date or an address with a letter or digit right before or after it does not count. Save the regex alone in {WS}/regex.txt: we read the file, strip it, and run re.findall(pattern, log_text, re.MULTILINE) over a whole log, expecting exactly those dates in order."
 ],
 "answers": [
  "[goal] One regex capturing the last valid calendar date on every log line containing an IPv4 address.\n\n[action] Verified `regex.txt` against synthetic edge cases and `server.log`. The regex:\n\n`^(?=[^\\n]*(?<![A-Za-z0-9])(?:0|[1-9][0-9]?|1[0-9][0-9]|2[0-4][0-9]|25[0-5])(?:\\.(?:0|[1-9][0-9]?|1[0-9][0-9]|2[0-4][0-9]|25[0-5])){3}(?![A-Za-z0-9]))[^\\n]*(?<![A-Za-z0-9])((?:[0-9]{4})-(?:(?:01|03|05|07|08|10|12)-(?:0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])|(?:04|06|09|11)-(?:0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|30)|02-(?:0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|29)))(?![A-Za-z0-9])[^\\n]*$`\n\n[result] `re.findall` over `server.log` returns `['2026-04-01', '2026-04-05', '2026-04-02']`, the last valid date on each line that contains an address (lines 1, 3, 5); line 2 has no address, line 4 has no address, so nothing.\n\nAssumptions: any 4-digit year is acceptable (year 0000-9999), February allowed the 29th in every year as stated, no leap-year check. \"Last date\" = rightmost valid date; a date preceded or followed by a letter or digit is excluded. Line-anchored `^...$` matching means one output per address-bearing line, in file order."
 ],
